/**
 * @page guides-spatial-matrix Matrix solutions and spatial tensors
 *
 * @section spatial-matrix-model Mathematical range and ordering
 *
 * P0, P0g, P1 and H1 accept @c Math::SpatialMatrix<Scalar> ranges for
 * @c Real and @c Complex entries. Rows and columns are explicit, independently
 * between one and @c RODIN_MAXIMAL_SPACE_DIMENSION. Each matrix entry is represented independently.
 * A matrix space is the tensor product of its scalar space with the matrix
 * units: @f$ V_h^{m\times n}=V_h\otimes\mathbb{K}^{m\times n} @f$.
 * Matrix specializations live beside the scalar and vector specializations
 * in each space family. Reference bases, physical mappings and DOF distribution
 * belong to that family. The componentwise construction here describes P0,
 * P0g, P1 and H1; future @f$H(\mathrm{div})@f$ and @f$H(\mathrm{curl})@f$
 * families must supply their own conforming bases and Piola mappings.
 * Local and global coefficient indices are interleaved in row-major order:
 * @f$ a(mn)+in+j @f$. No Voigt scaling or symmetry reduction is applied.
 * This coefficient numbering is independent of matrix memory storage;
 * @ref guides-ordering "Matrix storage and finite-element component ordering"
 * describes both conventions and their consequences for assembly.
 *
 * @code{.cpp}
 * H1 stressSpace(std::integral_constant<size_t, 3>{}, mesh, 3, 3);
 * GridFunction stress(stressSpace);
 * TrialFunction sigma(stressSpace);
 * TestFunction tau(stressSpace);
 * BilinearForm mass(sigma, tau);
 * mass = Integral(sigma, tau);
 * @endcode
 *
 * @section spatial-matrix-operators Differential and constitutive operators
 *
 * Inner products use @f$ A:B=\sum_{ij}A_{ij}\overline{B_{ij}} @f$.
 * Grad and Jacobian return @c SpatialTensor<Scalar,3> with
 * @f$ G_{ijk}=\partial_k A_{ij} @f$. Derivative selects its last axis.
 * Div uses row-wise divergence @f$ (\operatorname{div}A)_i=\sum_j\partial_jA_{ij} @f$
 * and requires the column count to match the ambient spatial dimension.
 * A matrix coefficient multiplying a matrix solution acts on its rows;
 * a fourth-order tensor coefficient couples arbitrary matrix entries:
 * @f$ (\mathcal C A)_{ij}=\sum_{kl}\mathcal C_{ijkl}A_{kl} @f$.
 * TensorFunction wraps constant or callable tensor coefficients. Tensor rank
 * is a compile-time parameter; extents are runtime values with stack storage.
 * The solution API supports scalar, vector and matrix ranges.
 * A linear form must have a scalar integrand. Bare @c Integral(v) is therefore
 * rejected for vector- and matrix-valued test functions; @c Integral(F,v)
 * contracts a matching coefficient with the test function to form a scalar.
 *
 * Matrix Jacobians have rank three. The vector-only P1 Jacobian handlers and
 * the weighted, rank-one and advection H1 handlers are restricted to their
 * supported ranges. Matrix expressions use the existing generic quadrature
 * rule for these forms; the componentwise unweighted H1 Jacobian handler
 * supports matrix ranges directly. An explicit quadrature order remains
 * appropriate for rational pyramid bases and curved mappings.
 *
 * @section spatial-matrix-nonlocal Nonlocal P1 forms
 *
 * The existing P1 potential quadrature handler accepts matrix trial and test
 * functions. A kernel returning a scalar multiplies every component. A kernel
 * filling a @c SpatialMatrix acts on rows, while a kernel filling a
 * @c SpatialTensor<Scalar,4> couples all entries:
 * @f[
 *   (K A)_{ij}(x)=\int_\Omega
 *      \sum_{kl}\mathcal K_{ijkl}(x,y)A_{kl}(y)\,dy.
 * @f]
 * The callback signatures are @c kernel(x,y), @c kernel(matrix,x,y) and
 * @c kernel(tensor,x,y), respectively. Output extents must match the field
 * dimensions. The existing collapsed quadrature on coincident triangles and
 * centroid rules on other panel pairs are retained. Singular kernels require
 * accuracy checks for their particular quadrature treatment. The P1 nonlocal
 * handler is covered by local Eigen and OpenMP regression tests. The MPI
 * tests cover Jacobian and reaction–diffusion assembly separately.
 *
 * @code{.cpp}
 * Math::SpatialTensor<Real, 4> elasticity(3, 3, 3, 3);
 * elasticity.setZero();
 * // Fill the constitutive entries, then assemble without a Voigt conversion.
 * auto constitutive = Integral(TensorFunction(elasticity) * sigma, tau);
 * @endcode
 *
 * Callable MatrixFunction coefficients accept explicit dimensions and
 * @c setOrder for polynomial quadrature selection. Integrator @c setOrder
 * specifies the integration degree. Rational pyramid bases and curved
 * transformations require quadrature chosen for the mapped integrand;
 * accuracy is assessed for the mapped integrand.
 * Grid-function derivatives on interfaces require a @c traceOf domain, as
 * for scalar fields. Shape-function derivatives on a boundary are derivatives
 * of the boundary element, matching the scalar finite element semantics.
 *
 * @section spatial-matrix-backends Backend and format support
 *
 * The same range and DOF maps are consumed by Eigen dense/sparse sequential
 * and OpenMP assembly and by local/distributed PETSc assembly. MPI spaces
 * expand scalar ownership and ghost maps component-wise. They cache collective
 * global sizes at construction, keeping local evaluations noncollective.
 *
 * HDF5 persistence preserves raw coefficients, matrix rows/columns and complex
 * imaginary parts. PETSc persistence stores the owned portion per shard.
 * XDMF writes full matrix-valued arrays, including nonsymmetric entries.
 * MFEM uses the scalar family's node conversion for each matrix entry; matrix
 * dimensions must be supplied by the destination space because MFEM records
 * only component count. Its existing unsupported collections remain unsupported
 * (in particular the global constant P0g collection).
 * MEDIT uses separate scalar vertex fields for every entry. Vertex formats
 * interpolate higher-order fields from vertices and cannot preserve their
 * arbitrary interior coefficients; use HDF5 for exact persistence.
 *
 * @section spatial-matrix-validation Validation coverage
 *
 * Native tests exercise all eight reference geometries, every matrix shape
 * between one and three axes, nodal duality through cubic H1 and additional
 * fourth/fifth-order representatives. Assembly and differential tests cover
 * every non-point cell geometry, P0/P0g/P1/H1, rectangular and square ranges,
 * complex entries, fourth-order material coupling, physical finite differences,
 * curved and embedded triangles, boundary traces, cache lifetime, and Dirichlet
 * data. Manufactured reaction-diffusion tests exercise complete solve paths.
 * MPI/PETSc tests exercise ownership, ghosts, assembly and solves at one through
 * four ranks. IO tests check real/complex HDF5, MEDIT vertex fields, MFEM node
 * permutations and XDMF matrix metadata. These tests define the current regression coverage. Further combinations
 * require corresponding numerical verification.
 */
